title: GUI file endpoints
tags: [[file panel]]
kind: reference
audience: dev
aliases: fsraw thumbnail
summary: The routes of the GUI's server that hand files to panels — /fsraw, /thumbnail, /document, and the two media probes — their caches and what a failure means.

Served by the GUI's own server on the loopback (`crates/gui/src/server/`), for panels:
the web view cannot read a local file. All but `/__media-support` need the session
token, and accept it as `?token=` — an `<img>` or `<video>` source cannot send a
header ([[Session tokens]]). `path` is an absolute path, in the escaped form the
panels are given ([[Tree names]]).

|!Route |!Answers |
|`GET /fsraw?path=` |the file's bytes, with `Range` support (seeking in a video, the first bytes of a text) |
|`GET /thumbnail?path=` |a still PNG, 320 px wide: a frame of a video, the first frame of a GIF (`ffmpeg`), the first page of a PDF (poppler) |
|`GET /document?path=&page=N&dpi=D` |page `N` (from 1) of a PDF as a PNG; `dpi` clamped to 20–600, 150 when absent |
|`GET /document/info?path=` |`{"pages": n}` |
|`GET /__media-support` |`{audio, video, missing}` — whether the output elements WebKit needs exist |
|`GET /__media-probe?path=` |`{missing, slow}` — the decoders this file lacks, or a warning that the one it would get is too slow |

The panels reach them through `thumbnail()` of `/__ui.js` and the [[file panel]]
([[The metafolder API]]).

!! Media, never a document

`/fsraw` is safe only as the source of an `<img>`, `<video>` or `<audio>`
([[Why /fsraw never serves a document]]). Hence two rules of `thumbnail()`:

* a video never goes to an `<img>` through `/fsraw` — WebKit fetches the whole file, tries to decode it as an image, and its process grows to gigabytes and dies. A video tile points at `/thumbnail`.
* a GIF tile points at `/thumbnail` too: served raw it animates, and a grid of animations is noise. If the still cannot be made, the tile falls back to the animated original, then to the glyph.

`/fsraw`, `/thumbnail` and `/document` responses carry
`Content-Security-Policy: sandbox; default-src 'none'; …` and `nosniff`.

!! The caches

`/thumbnail` and `/document` write what they render inside the repository holding the
file: `<internal>/thumbnails/` and `<internal>/documents/`
([[Repository layout on disk]]). The cache is the repository's, and goes with it.

* The repository is found from the daemon's `GET /repos` — each `root` and `internal_dir`, the longest root that contains the path — never by walking the disk. Nested repositories give the innermost, an external `.metafolder` is handled, and a stray `.metafolder/` directory is not taken for one. The list is kept `repo-list-cache-ttl-secs` (3 s), or a grid would ask once per tile.
* A file inside no loaded repository gets `404` and nothing is written. `/document/info` writes nothing, and answers for any readable file.
* The name of a cached file hashes the source's path, modification time and size — and the page and resolution, for a document — so a changed file is rendered again. The helper writes into a scratch directory, and the one PNG expected is moved in.

!! Failures

A non-2xx answer means ''show a glyph'' to a tile and a message to the preview; the
status is for whoever reads the network log.

|!Status |!Means |
|`415` |the file's content is not of a kind this route renders |
|`404` |no such file, or a file inside no loaded repository |
|`500` |the helper failed: not installed, no sandbox, a file it cannot read, a timeout (20 s) |

A missing file is `404` before its type is looked at.

!! What a file is

Every decision above is taken on the file's ''content'', never its extension
(`crates/gui/src/file_kind.rs`): the first 8 KiB go through the `infer` crate — magic
bytes, the detector behind the daemon's `mfr_mime` ([[File metadata fields]]), so the
GUI and the repository agree — and give one of five kinds, or none.

|!Kind |!Is |
|`image` |JPEG, PNG, WebP, BMP, AVIF, ICO — what an `<img>` draws — and SVG |
|`gif` |a GIF: an image shown as a still |
|`video` |any `video/*` type, an MPEG transport stream, a 3GPP file, and a container brand `ffprobe` found media in (below) |
|`audio` |any `audio/*` type |
|`document` |a PDF |

Three formats `infer` does not know are added. An MPEG transport stream is three packets
in a row opening with the sync byte `0x47`, 188 bytes apart, or 192 for a Blu-ray
`.m2ts` — one `G` at the start of a text is not enough. A 3GPP file, what a phone
records, is an MP4-family container whose `ftyp` box names a `3gp…` or `3g2…` brand:
`infer` matches a closed list of MP4 brands, which has neither. Only those brands are
added, not every `ftyp` container — a Canon raw file is one too. An SVG is text, with
no magic bytes: it must be named `.svg` ''and'' hold an `<svg` element.

!! A container brand nobody listed

An MP4-family file names its ''brand'' in its `ftyp` box (`isom`, `3gp5`, `XAVC`…), and
`infer` matches a closed list of them: a video of any other brand had no kind, and
opened on ''no preview available''. A longer list only moves the hole. So when `infer`
knows nothing of a file that has an `ftyp` box, the first file of that brand is handed
to `ffprobe` — sandboxed, like every program that reads your files
([[Sandboxing the media decoders]]) — and the answer is kept ''per brand'', not per
file: a folder of a thousand such files costs one probe, once.

* The answers are kept in `~/.local/state/metafolder/gui/media-brands` (under `$XDG_STATE_HOME` when set), one line per brand: the brand, a tab, `video` or `none`. It is not in the configuration repository — the GUI writes it by itself, and nothing there is written at run time. It is yours to edit: change a line to correct an answer, delete it to have the brand asked again. It is read when the GUI starts.
* A brand is a `video` as soon as `ffprobe` names the codec of one audio or video stream. One answer stands for every file of the brand, and a brand holds films and voice memos alike; in doubt it is the video player, which plays an audio-only file — the audio player would show no picture.
* A probe that fails — no `ffmpeg` package, a truncated file — teaches nothing: that file has no kind, and the next one of its brand is asked. One broken file does not settle its brand.
* Only what `infer` does not know is asked about. A HEIF picture is a type it names, deliberately not an image here, and `ffprobe` would find a video stream in it.
* A file without an `ftyp` box has no brand, so nothing to keep an answer under: it is never probed, and stays ''no preview available'' ([[file panel]]).

`/thumbnail` makes a poster for a `video`, a `gif` and a `document`; `/document*`
answers for a `document`. A panel gets the kind from `metafolder.fs.stat(path).kind`
([[The metafolder API]]), which is how the [[file panel]] chooses its preview.

Extensions were used before, from lists kept in three places — the panel's
configuration, `thumbnails.rs`, `documents.rs` — which could disagree, and a name
says little: `.ts` is a transport stream or a TypeScript source. One trace is left:
`thumbnail()` still picks the ''route'' a tile tries by extension, a grid being unable
to pay one round-trip per entry to ask. It is a hint: the server answers `415` for
what the content is not, and the tile shows its glyph.

Reading those bytes runs in the GUI's own process, unlike the decoders: it compares
bytes and decodes nothing ([[Sandboxing the media decoders]]).

!! The two probes

`/__media-support` runs `gst-inspect-1.0 --exists` on `autoaudiosink` and
`autovideosink`, once per process; without the tool it looks for
`libgstautodetect.so` in the plugin directories. What cannot be determined counts as
''missing''. Video needs both: a soundtrack is the rule.

`/__media-probe` runs `gst-discoverer-1.0` on the file, sandboxed, with a 15 s
timeout, and remembers the answer per path and modification time. `missing` lists the
descriptions under the tool's ''Missing plugins''. `slow` is set only when nothing is
missing ([[Why only measured codecs warn as slow]]). No sandbox, no tool or a
timeout: an empty answer, and the player is tried.
